The closest known approach of Apophis will occur on 13 April 2029, at 21:46 [[Universal Time|UT]], when Apophis will pass Earth at a distance of about {{convert|31600|km|lk=on}} above the surface.<ref name="surface"/> Using the June 2024 orbit solution which includes the [[Yarkovsky effect]], the [[68–95–99.7 rule|3-sigma]] uncertainty region in the 2029 approach distance is about ±3.3&nbsp;km.<ref name="Horizons2029"/><ref name="jpldata"/> The distance, a hair's breadth in astronomical terms, is five times the [[Earth radius|radius of the Earth]], one tenth the distance to the [[Moon]],<ref name="blog2023"/> and closer than the ring of [[Geostationary orbit|geostationary]] satellites currently orbiting the Earth.<ref name="satsig"/><ref name="earthsky-preparing"/> It will be the closest asteroid of its size in recorded history. On that date, it will become as bright as magnitude&nbsp;3.1<ref name="NEODyS2029"/> (visible to the [[naked eye]] from rural as well as darker suburban areas, visible with [[binoculars]] from most locations).<ref name="mag-scale"/> The close approach will be visible from [[Europe]], [[Africa]], and western [[Asia]]. Over the course of about a day, Apophis will move northwest from [[Centaurus]] to [[Perseus (constellation)|Perseus]] and then southwest to [[Pisces (constellation)|Pisces]], an arc of 205°.<ref name="sky_arc"/> Approaching Earth its speed relative to Earth will be 6.0&nbsp;km/s. Earth's gravity will accelerate it to 7.4&nbsp;km/s at the time of closest approach, and then slow it back down to 6 as it departs.<ref name="deceleration_JPL"/> During the approach, Earth will [[Perturbation (astronomy)|perturb]] Apophis from an [[Aten asteroid|Aten]]-class orbit with a [[Semi-major and semi-minor axes|semi-major axis]] of 0.92&nbsp;[[Astronomical unit|AU]] to an [[Apollo asteroid|Apollo]]-class orbit with a semi-major axis of 1.1&nbsp;AU.<ref name="Orbit2029"/> [[Apsis|Perihelion]] will lift from 0.746&nbsp;AU to 0.895&nbsp;AU and aphelion will lift from 1.10&nbsp;AU to 1.31&nbsp;AU.<ref name="Orbit2029"/>

