No observations of Apophis were made between January 2015 and February 2019 but observations began again in January 2020.<ref name="mpc"/> In March 2020, astronomers [[David J. Tholen|David Tholen]] and Davide Farnocchia measured the acceleration of Apophis due to the [[Yarkovsky effect]] for the first time, significantly improving the prediction of its orbit past the 2029 flyby. Tholen and Farnocchia found that the Yarkovsky effect causes the [[Semi-major and semi-minor axes|semi-major axis]] to decrease by about 170 metres per year, causing an increase in [[Ecliptic coordinate system|ecliptic longitude]] that is [[Quadratic function|quadratic]] in time.<ref name="TholenFarmocchia"/> In late 2020, Apophis approached the Earth and passed {{convert|0.11265|AU|e6km LD|abbr=unit}} from Earth on 6 March 2021, brightening to +15 [[apparent magnitude|mag]] at that time. Radar observations of Apophis were carried out at Goldstone in March 2021.<ref name=Jorge/><ref name="Goldstone2021"/> The asteroid has been observed by [[Wide-field Infrared Survey Explorer|NEOWISE]] (between December 2020 and April 2021)<ref name="Satpathy2022"/><ref name="FrankfurterRundschau_20210304"/> and by [[Near Earth Object Surveillance Satellite|NEOSSat]] (in January 2021).<ref name="Reddy2022"/><ref name="IAWN2021"/><ref name="IAWN2021-Gallery"/>

These observations showed that the [[impact parameter]] ζ (basically how far behind Earth Apophis would pass if it were not deflected by the gravitational pull of Earth) in 2029 will be about 47,363&nbsp;km,<ref name="Jorge"/> less than the earlier nominal value of 47,659&nbsp;km by 296&nbsp;km because of the [[Yarkovsky effect]]. This means that Apophis will not hit Earth in the coming century, in particular avoiding the keyhole 212.14&nbsp;km below nominal that would have led to a collision in 2068.<ref name="Farnocchia2013_Icarus"/>

Apophis was the target of an observing campaign by the [[International Asteroid Warning Network]], resulting in the collection of [[light curve]]s, [[Astronomical spectroscopy|spectra]], and [[astrometry]].<ref name="Reddy2022"/><ref name="IAWN2021"/><ref name="IAWN2021-Gallery"/> The observations were used to practice and coordinate the response to an actual impact threat. Ignoring all earlier observations, the estimated probability of an impact in 2029 reached 16 percent before going down to zero.<ref name="PlanetaryDefense2021"/>

On 21 February 2021, Apophis was removed from the [[Sentry (monitoring system)|Sentry Risk Table]], as an impact in the next 100 years was finally ruled out.<ref name="removed"/><ref name="NASA_analysis_2021"/><ref name="Hurst2021"/>

The 2021 apparition was the last opportunity to observe Apophis until shortly before its 2029 flyby.<ref name="jpldata"/>

Entering March 2021, six asteroids each had a more notable cumulative [[Palermo scale]] rating than Apophis, and none of those has a Torino level above 0.<ref name="risk-table"/>{{efn|name=palermo}} In the longer term, Apophis will however continue to be a threat for possibly thousands of years, until it is removed from being a potentially hazardous object, for instance by passing close to Venus or Mars.{{citation needed|date=July 2026}}

==== Stellar occultations ====
Several [[occultation]]s of bright stars ([[apparent magnitude]] 8–11) by Apophis occurred in March and April 2021.<ref name="JOA2021_3"/><ref name="Gaia_20210329"/><ref name="Tanga2021"/><ref name="Occultation award"/> A total of five separate occultations were observed successfully, marking the first time that an asteroid as small as Apophis was observed using the [[Occultation|occultation method]] (beating the previous record set in 2019 by asteroid [[3200 Phaethon]], which is more than ten times the size of Apophis).<ref name="Gaia_20210329"/>

The first event, on 7 March, was successfully observed from the United States by multiple observers.<ref name="SETI2021"/><ref name="Occultation_prelim"/><ref name="JOA2021_3"/> The next potential occultation, which occurred on 11 March, was predicted to be visible from central Europe,<ref name="Tanga2021"/> but was not observed, mainly because of bad weather (two negative observations were recorded from [[Greece]]).<ref name="Gaia_20210329"/> Another occultation occurred on 22 March, but larger-than-expected [[Errors and residuals|residuals]] in the 7 March data had caused the majority of observers to be deployed outside of the actual path for the occultation,<ref name="JOA2021_3"/> and it was observed only by a single observer from the United States, [[amateur astronomy|amateur astronomer]] Roger Venable. This single detection then allowed the prediction of several more events that would have been unobservable otherwise, including an occultation on 4 April, which was observed from [[New Mexico]], again by Venable, alongside others.<ref name="Occultation award"/><ref name="JOA2021_3"/> Two more occultations, observable on 10 and 11 April from [[Japan]] and New Mexico, respectively, were seen by several observers each.<ref name="JOA2021_3"/> The occultation measurements helped refine the asteroid's size and orbit.<ref name="vvs.be"/>{{better source needed|date=July 2026}}

